  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Seasonably cool today into the upcoming week with\nthe bulk of showers from the next, fast-moving upper level \ndisturbance confined to the Northern Mountains of PA late \ntonight and Monday morning\n\nA weak ridge of high pressure at the surface and aloft will move\nover Pennsylvania today, leading to a gradual backing of the \nwind from the west to the southwest by the midday hours. \n\nNon-precipitating, shallow stratocu clouds blanketing much of \nthe region early today will clear out for about a 7 to 9 hour \nperiod this afternoon and evening before another fast moving \nupper level disturbance and weak cold front moves southeast from\nthe Great Lakes. This next weather feature will spread clouds \nacross the region later tonight into Monday morning with \noccasional showers focused primarily across the Northern \nMountains of Pennsylvania. Rainfall amounts of 1 to 2 tenths of \nan inch are likely across the Northwest Mountains of PA tonight \nand Monday Morning with much lighter amounts further to the \nSoutheast.\n\nA few brief showers could sneak into areas of the \nstate south of Interstate 80 late tonight and early Monday.\n\nOtherwise, it will be breezy and cooler through early in the \nupcoming week. Wind gusts today will range through the 20s (mph) \nbut northwest winds on Monday could gust as high as 35 and 40 \nmph. \n\nThere is a growing model signal for a more substantial long \nwave trough to carve out over the eastern U.S. by the end of \nthe week. While still very preliminary given the extended\nforecast length and associated uncertainly/low confidence, it\nappears the coldest air of the Fall season will attempt to\nexpand southward late next week with the upper trough and could\nbe a factor with a potential coastal storm supplying a swath of\nprecipitation including accumulating snow particularly in the\nhigher elevations.",
